  "rendered": "Among the aluminium-based entries in this table, the highest Li-ion diffusivity belongs to Li3.5Al2 O3 (am)[1], at 7.1 ×10^− 10[2] cm^2/s at 300[3] K. Amorphous Al2O3, listed as Al2 O3 (am)[4], has a diffusivity of 5.94 ×10^− 17[5] cm^2/s at the same temperature. So the lithiated amorphous aluminium oxide is many orders of magnitude more diffusive than amorphous Al2O3, which is among the least mobile oxides in the table. The other aluminium-based entries fall in between: LiAlO2 is at 1 ×10^− 11[6] cm^2/s, Li0.2Al2O3 at 1.1 ×10^− 14[7] cm^2/s, and the AlF3 forms at 9.26 ×10^− 16[8] (amorphous) and 6.2 ×10^− 18[9] (crystalline) cm^2/s. This fits the paper's remark that coating oxides with different lithium stoichiometry show higher lithium-ion diffusivity than the parent oxides.",
